## Documentation contract (follow on every change)

- Every app change → one line in [.memory/CHANGELOG.md](.memory/CHANGELOG.md).
  The docs-contract Stop gate holds the session until it moves.
- Every repo-structure/docs change → one line in
  [.memory/DOCS-CHANGELOG.md](.memory/DOCS-CHANGELOG.md).
- Architectural decision → one dated line in
  [.memory/DECISIONS.md](.memory/DECISIONS.md) with the why (`/decide`).
- Noticed but not acted on → [.memory/ISSUES.md](.memory/ISSUES.md).
- Speculative thought → [.memory/IDEAS.md](.memory/IDEAS.md). Never act on it unasked.
- Changed but not yet verifiable → [.memory/VERIFY.md](.memory/VERIFY.md)
  with acceptance criteria; drain via `/verify`.
- A decision made, a discussion that changed the plan, a surprise → append to
  this change's diary entry in `.memory/diary/` **as it happens** (`/diary`).
  One entry per branch/MR by default, so everything about a change stays
  together. Do not save it for session end: the pre-commit gate asks for it at
  the commit that carries the decision, and the Stop gate checks it again.
- A change that makes a `.memory/contexts/` or `.memory/references/` doc
  stale → update it in the same session (context_attach surfaces the paired
  doc; see `.memory/contexts/README.md`).

## Change policy (non-negotiable)

- **Protected branches (`protected_branches` in `.claude/kit.json` — main/
  master) are owner-only.** Claude never merges into them, never pushes to
  them, never `gh pr merge` — get the PR green (`/pr-babysit`) and wait for
  the owner. Enforced by guard_protected_merge; the rule stands even where a
  hook wouldn't catch it.
- Branch names: `<type>/<slug>`, types from `.claude/kit.json` `branch_types`.
- **No secrets in the repo, ever.** Claude never edits `.env*`, credentials,
  or lockfiles by hand — hand exact values/commands to the owner.
- Destructive ops (dropping data, force-push, resource deletion) need
  explicit approval first.
- Owner-stated always/never rules get written to
  [.memory/CONVENTIONS.md](.memory/CONVENTIONS.md) in the same session — and
  encoded as a red test per
  [.memory/references/engineering-principles.md](.memory/references/engineering-principles.md).
- **A process another EXISTING repo could implement today belongs in the
  ecosystem kit, not here** (owner rule 2026-08-01). The bar is *more than one*
  repo, not every repo — and it is present-tense. Before closing work that
  built or fixed a process, recipe, guard, script or runbook, ask it out loud:
  **which existing repo could implement this right now, or already has its own
  version?** Name it, then propose the promotion (`/retro` step 4) — never edit
  the kit from a project session. If you cannot name a repo, it stays here: a
  future need is not a reason, and the promotion happens when that repo
  actually needs it. Another repo already having its own copy is the strongest
  case — that means the promotion is overdue. Applicability is not
  universality: something suiting two repos still belongs in the kit, gated by
  relevance where it is used. Genuinely project-bound things — this repo's
  schema, business rules, infrastructure names — stay here.

